1:# Decision Log — schema decisions, with the why
9:## 2026-11-22 (rates, -30 r4) — the plain read of a premise's places reads what is committed; round-4 findings settled by standing rule
15:## 2026-11-22 (scenarios) — Ryan: the amount the utility's error hid is one charge, part of it due later, not a matched pair of entries
19:## 2026-11-22 (rates, -30 r3) — a past read refuses a place with two different answers and gives one answer for the same place twice; round-3 findings settled by standing rule
25:## 2026-11-22 (scenarios) — Ryan: a corrected bill shows the full true figure; what the utility's error hid is moved to the next bill by a matched pair of lines
29:## 2026-11-22 (scenarios) — Ryan: on the full-amount penalty base, whether an unpaid bill takes the penalty once or every month is the city's own setting, with no default; Pecan Flat takes it every month
33:## 2026-11-22 (money, -25) — Ryan: the silent-city dispute default stops the late fee and both disconnection steps
37:## 2026-11-22 (money, -25) — Ryan: the silent-city dispute default reaches every customer
41:## 2026-11-22 (scenarios) — Ryan: when the silent-city default's 60 days end with the dispute unresolved, the disputed amount moves to the next bill's due date
46:## 2026-11-22 (money, -25) — Ryan: of the two dispute rulings of 2026-11-07, the scenarios version stands
51:## 2026-11-07 (scenarios) — Ryan: a silent city's dispute default protects the disputed amount only
55:## 2026-11-07 (scenarios) — Ryan: a city's own dispute rule wins over Kyle's "no penalty while a dispute is open"
59:## 2026-11-07 (scenarios) — Ryan: where a city records no dispute rule, a dispute raised before the due date protects the bill until resolved or 60 days after issue
64:## 2026-11-07 (scenarios) — Ryan: Tally's reading of a utility's records is logged by the application and shown to the utility; refused attempts go to operational logs
68:## 2026-11-07 (scenarios) — Ryan: every utility has a hidden Tally User; Tally staff act in a utility by impersonating it
72:## 2026-11-07 (money, -25 r1 review) — Ryan: no late penalty on a bill while its dispute is open is a platform rule the penalty engine applies
78:## 2026-11-07 (money, -24 r1 review) — Ryan: the two places the refund-to-the-agency rule is not database-enforced stay known limits; round-1 findings settled by standing rule
83:## 2026-11-07 (money, -24 design) — Ryan: a refund of an agency's money goes to the agency, for every agency
88:## 2026-11-06 (rates, -30 r2) — an overlapping state correction retries; round-2 findings settled by standing rule
94:## 2026-11-06 (rates, -30 r1) — round-1 findings settled by standing rule; a premise's state recorded on change; Kyle's franchise answers in
102:## 2026-11-06 (-27) — Ryan: a short round 5 before the mirror; Codex waived for it; -27 mirrored
108:## 2026-11-06 (-27 r3) — an undo is dated no later than today; the database records which undo gave money to an escheated credit
113:## 2026-11-05 (rates, tax) — Ryan: the franchise base goes to Kyle; tax work split in three; -30's drafting calls
120:## 2026-11-05 (rates, -29) — Ryan: Codex reviews -29 (not waived), all three re-review round 3, and -29 is mirrored
126:## 2026-11-05 (-27 r1) — Ryan: undoing a repayment whose credit was since escheated carries the money into a new credit
130:## 2026-11-05 (-27 design) — a refund that comes back after its shortfall was paid waits until the netting is undone
134:## 2026-11-05 (scenarios) — Kyle's answers to the second scenario batch folded into the scenarios
138:## 2026-11-05 (scenarios) — Ryan: a billing run reads every bill at one recorded instant, fixed when the run starts
144:## 2026-11-05 (scenarios) — Ryan: every link between two utility tables proves both rows belong to the same utility (R-12, option 1)
148:## 2026-11-05 (scenarios) — Ryan: Tally employees may impersonate a utility's users, on Tally's own decision; their plain access is read-only
153:## 2026-11-05 (scenarios) — Ryan: a backbill for a customer outside Texas's protected class is refused until the utility's tariff states a rule
158:## 2026-11-04 (scenarios) — Ryan: a gas bill line carries the conversion figures only when it is billed in an energy unit
162:## 2026-11-04 (scenarios) — Kyle's answers to the first scenario batch folded into the scenarios
166:## 2026-11-04 (-23 r9) — a reissue or overpayment credit is not held to the payment family's cap
170:## 2026-11-04 (-28) — mirrored ahead of the money stream's three patches
174:## 2026-11-04 (-28 r2) — the fee's state-agency check keeps reading the bill's time zone; every Texas county is loaded once as shared data
178:## 2026-11-02 (-28 r1) — percentage rates are stored as a percent to four decimals; entered and shown however the utility writes them
182:## 2026-11-02 (-28 r1) — a consolidated bill is fixed once it goes out: every member bill final at issue, nothing attaches after (money stream's rule)
186:## 2026-11-02 (-23 r8) — AC-44's netting: per-state before an escheat report; recorded as an application to the shortfall (-27)
192:## 2026-11-02 (scenarios) — Ryan: the scenario cast is modelled on real small-city gas tariffs, under invented names
196:## 2026-11-02 (scenarios) — Ryan: one scenario is a rule line, Given / When / Then, and a worked bill
200:## 2026-11-02 (scenarios) — Ryan: scenarios are written in this repo, in `scenarios/`
